WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.047
Received bytes should be treated as untrusted input.

2
00:00:03.047 --> 00:00:12.999
A socket parser should enforce message size limits, field types, numeric
ranges, allowed commands, expected state, and business rules before data
reaches sensitive logic.

3
00:00:12.999 --> 00:00:20.243
The parser should reject invalid messages predictably and avoid revealing
unnecessary internal details in error responses.

4
00:00:20.243 --> 00:00:22.680
Input validation is not only for web forms.

5
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.253
It is fundamental to every network protocol.

6
00:00:25.253 --> 00:00:30.669
Unsafe deserialization and overly flexible parsing are common design
hazards at a high level.

7
00:00:30.669 --> 00:00:39.538
If a protocol accepts complex objects, expression-like data, or
dynamically interpreted content from a peer, the application has to
understand the risk.

8
00:00:39.538 --> 00:00:45.835
Simpler formats with explicit fields and allowlisted commands are easier
to validate, log, and reason about.

9
00:00:45.835 --> 00:00:49.490
The protocol should make unsafe behavior difficult to express.

10
00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:51.792
Resource limits protect availability.

11
00:00:51.792 --> 00:01:01.271
A peer may send oversized messages, send data slowly, open many
connections, trigger expensive parsing paths, or create backpressure
through slow reads and writes.

12
00:01:01.271 --> 00:01:09.463
The server and client should define limits for message size, connection
count, memory use, CPU-intensive operations, and time spent waiting.

13
00:01:09.463 --> 00:01:12.238
These controls are not only performance tuning.

14
00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:15.150
They define how the protocol behaves under stress.

15
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.332
Safe error responses should be consistent and limited.

16
00:01:18.332 --> 00:01:28.826
The peer can be told that a message is invalid, unauthorized, too large,
or unsupported without receiving stack traces, secrets, internal object
names, or detailed parser internals.

17
00:01:28.826 --> 00:01:32.617
Logging can preserve safe diagnostic information for operators.

18
00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:42.840
A well-designed parser fails closed, preserves resources, and gives
legitimate clients enough information to recover without helping untrusted
peers map the implementation.
